---------------------Phase 1 — Project Planning and MVP Definition--------------------------

The project initially began as a digital attendance system designed to replace traditional manual attendance methods. The original concept used QR codes to allow students to check into an active class session.

After reviewing the concept with the professor, the team identified a major weakness in the QR-based approach: a QR code could potentially be shared with another student, allowing someone who was not physically present to receive attendance credit.

Because preventing attendance fraud is one of the primary goals of T.A.P., the team decided to remove QR-code attendance from the system and redesign the identification process around facial recognition.

    The revised MVP workflow became:

        Instructor Creates Session
                    ↓
        Student Selects Active Session
                    ↓
        Student Captures Live Face Image
                    ↓
        Backend Receives Image
                    ↓
        Exadel CompreFace Performs Recognition
                    ↓
        Backend Verifies Student + Enrollment
                    ↓
        Duplicate Check
                    ↓
        Attendance Recorded in PostgreSQL
                    ↓
        Instructor Can View Attendance

The team selected Exadel CompreFace as the open-source facial-recognition platform for the MVP.

This change required revisions to the project's use-case model, system architecture, database design, backend responsibilities, and frontend requirements.

--------------------------Phase 2 — System Architecture and Backend Planning-----------------------------

Before beginning implementation, the backend responsibilities were separated from the facial-recognition service itself.

The backend was designed around:

Frontend
   ↓
Node.js / Express REST API
   ↓
Business Logic
   ├────→ Exadel CompreFace
   │
   └────→ PostgreSQL

A major architectural decision was to avoid tightly coupling the attendance system to CompreFace.

Instead, facial recognition was isolated into:

src/services/compreface.service.js

This means the rest of T.A.P. only needs to request a recognition result rather than understanding how CompreFace internally processes an image.

This also allows the backend, frontend, database, and facial-recognition components to be developed independently and integrated later.

