-------------------------Phase 4 — Development Environment Problems and Solutions-------------------------------

Several configuration issues were encountered while setting up the backend locally.

    Problem 1 — npm Was Not Recognized

        The first attempt to install project dependencies resulted in:

        npm : The term 'npm' is not recognized
    Cause

        Node.js/npm had not yet been correctly installed or made available through the Windows PATH.

    Resolution

        Node.js was installed and the installation was verified with:

            node --version
            npm --version

    Problem 2 — PowerShell Blocked npm

        After Node.js became available, npm produced:

            npm.ps1 cannot be loaded because running scripts is disabled on this system
        Cause

            Windows PowerShell's execution policy prevented the npm PowerShell script from running.

        Resolution

            The execution policy for the current Windows user was changed to:

                Set-ExecutionPolicy -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned -Scope CurrentUser

                After restarting the terminal, both Node and npm operated correctly.

    Problem 3 — Dependency Warnings
  
          Running:

              npm install

              successfully installed approximately 205 packages, but npm reported several deprecated packages and security warnings.

          The installation itself completed successfully.

          Rather than blindly applying automatic dependency changes during initial configuration, the decision was made to first establish a stable development environment and then review/update affected dependencies deliberately.

--------------------------Phase 5 — PostgreSQL Installation and Configuration--------------------------------

PostgreSQL was selected as the relational database for T.A.P.

During installation:

Port: 5432
Database administrator: postgres
Password: locally configured

The default PostgreSQL port of 5432 was retained.

Optional Stack Builder packages were not installed because they were unnecessary for the MVP.

Problem — psql Was Not Recognized

PostgreSQL installed successfully, but:

psql --version

initially returned:

The term 'psql' is not recognized

PostgreSQL itself was functional; Windows simply did not know where the command-line executable was located.

Resolution

The PostgreSQL binary directory was added to the Windows PATH:

C:\Program Files\PostgreSQL\18\bin

After restarting VS Code:

psql --version

successfully returned PostgreSQL 18.6.

The database server was then accessed using:

psql -U postgres

and the project's development database was created:

CREATE DATABASE tap;

----------------------Phase 6 — Database Schema Implementation--------------------------

Instead of manually creating database tables, SQL migrations were created so every team member can reproduce the same database structure.

Six initial migrations were implemented:

001_create_users.sql
002_create_courses.sql
003_create_enrollments.sql
004_create_class_sessions.sql
005_create_face_profiles.sql
006_create_attendance_records.sql

These migrations created the primary MVP entities:

users
courses
enrollments
class_sessions
face_profiles
attendance_records

A particularly important database rule was established for attendance:

UNIQUE(student_id, session_id)

This provides database-level protection against a student receiving multiple attendance records for the same class session.

Face recognition references are separated into face_profiles, allowing T.A.P. to associate a student with the recognition system without requiring attendance records themselves to contain biometric data.

